</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Automatic Tagger Evaluations</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the predicted cultural tags on a sample of 100 prompts each from a benchmark labeled with one of the cultural dimensions (Knowledge/Dynamics/Bias/Preference) from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. We expect the highest number of tags to agree with the benchmark label, but since these cultural dimensions are in practice not mutually exclusive, there are valid ambiguities as well.
The results in <a href="#A1.T8" title="In A.3 Data Release ‣ Appendix A Tagging Details ‣ The Culture Funnel: You Can’t Align What isn’t in the Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> confirm that the large majority of sample tags aligns with the benchmark label (68%–100% recall).
For BBQ and CIVICS, the predictions of the tagger are more dispersed than expected. <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NoCulture</em> is chosen for 21% samples of BBQ, where we would have expected <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Culture as Bias</em>. Upon inspection, it becomes clear that the label assigned on the dataset-level by  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al. (2024)</a></cite> does not necessarily apply to every single instance within the dataset, as the prompts in BBQ were also not designed with culture as primary axes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
